Parts of Theia's writing checks (ryoku/rashin/internal/writing/) are adapted
from seven projects. Selected English clarity terms come from write-good by
Brian Ford (https://github.com/btford/write-good) and its dependencies
weasel-words by Brian Ford (https://github.com/btford/weasel-words) and
too-wordy by Matt Blair (https://github.com/duereg/too-wordy). Selected
inclusive-wording terms come from retext-equality by Titus Wormer
(https://github.com/retextjs/retext-equality) and blocklint by Troy Comi
(https://github.com/PrincetonUniversity/blocklint). The reading ease and grade
formulas are the Flesch and Flesch-Kincaid ones as documented by textstat by
Shivam Bansal (https://github.com/textstat/textstat). Selected cliches come
from proselint's garner list by Jordan Suchow, Michael Pacer and Lara A. Ross
(https://github.com/amperser/proselint). They are modified versions: the terms
are curated and rewritten as Go data, and the matchers, syllable estimate and
machine-writing signals are Ryoku's own. All but proselint are released under
the MIT License and proselint under the BSD 3-Clause License; ryoku/rashin/NOTICE
carries each text with its copyright lines and the ryoku-rashin package
installs it as /usr/share/licenses/ryoku-rashin/NOTICE. Those licenses are
compatible with Ryoku's GPL-3 distribution.

The built-in English checker in ryoku/rashin/internal/grammar/ is a Go port of
Harper 3.2.0 by Automattic and the Harper contributors
(https://github.com/Automattic/harper), pinned at commit
ea9ab1b8f95be99a5d4040c53be92a6c2ad7f7f3. It is a modified version: Harper's
Rust grammar engine is rewritten in Go and runs inside Rashin, without the
Harper CLI or a separate process. Copyright 2024 Automattic Inc. Harper is
released under Apache-2.0; the license is in ryoku/rashin/LICENSE.apache-2.0
and installed as /usr/share/licenses/ryoku-rashin/LICENSE.apache-2.0. This
license is compatible with Ryoku's GPL-3 distribution.

The dictionary data bundled from that Harper commit are
harper-core/dictionary.dict, annotations.json, irregular_nouns.json and
irregular_verbs.json, shipped unchanged. Harper's dictionary began with the
SCOWL-derived en_US Hunspell dictionary 2020.12.07 from LibreOffice dictionaries
(https://github.com/LibreOffice/dictionaries/tree/master/en), imported at
Harper commit ecb2534e80e63442ada23183dc8028df1d9ea329. The original dictionary
blob b41fd203e6daf76934db99dec6572a230516368a matches LibreOffice's en/en_US.dic.
Its word lists carry the SCOWL, VarCon, WordNet and Ispell notices, not only
Harper's Apache license. The full supporting notice from
https://raw.githubusercontent.com/LibreOffice/dictionaries/master/en/README_en_US.txt
is reproduced in ryoku/rashin/NOTICE and installed as
/usr/share/licenses/ryoku-rashin/NOTICE.

The word identity hash in ryoku/rashin/internal/grammar/dict/word_id.go is a Go
adaptation of foldhash 0.2.0 by Orson Peters (https://github.com/orlp/foldhash),
using its numeric sponge and constants to preserve Harper's dictionary ids.
It is a modified version, not the original Rust implementation. Copyright
(c) 2024 Orson Peters. The Zlib license notice is reproduced in
ryoku/rashin/NOTICE and installed as /usr/share/licenses/ryoku-rashin/NOTICE;
the license is compatible with Ryoku's GPL-3 distribution.

Theia runs these programs as separate tools and ships none of their code.
eSpeak NG (https://github.com/espeak-ng/espeak-ng, GPL-3.0-or-later), by the
eSpeak NG authors, reads answers aloud. Piper
(https://github.com/rhasspy/piper, MIT), by Michael Hansen, is an optional
second voice engine for voices a user adds; each voice carries its own license.
yt-dlp (https://github.com/yt-dlp/yt-dlp, Unlicense), by the yt-dlp
